# .gitattributes - fork protection for Foundation and its forks.
#
# Foundation is meant to be forked. Your fork keeps its own version of the files listed below while
# still merging engine, command and documentation improvements from upstream. The "ours" merge
# driver makes `git merge foundation/master` (or Merge-Upstreams) keep YOUR copy of a listed file
# whenever both you and upstream changed it. Git only runs a merge driver when both sides changed
# the file: a listed file you never edited simply follows upstream (it is still the generic one)
# until you do, and an upstream deletion of a file you edited is a modify/delete conflict you
# resolve by hand. Mirrored and root files of a fork of both WinuX and Foundation need more than
# this: Merge-Upstreams (docs/foundation/fork-model.md).
#
# The driver must be registered before git runs it: Foundation's git overrides
# (Git/Unix/gitconfig.local, linked to ~/.gitconfig.local by chezmoi) set merge.ours.driver, so a
# bootstrapped Mac needs nothing more. Without the bootstrap, run once:
#   git config --global merge.ours.driver true
# Git never registers a merge driver from a cloned repository (a security boundary).
#
# Personal CONFIG needs no attribute: the base Unix/config/NAME.conf files stay generic and SHOULD
# track upstream, and your values go in the NAME.local.conf overlays, which upstream never tracks.
#
# NOTE for maintainers: these rules apply to this repository too, so a LOCAL merge of a pull request
# that edits one of these files keeps the repository's version. Merge such pull requests on GitHub,
# or reconcile by hand.

# Payloads a tool reads whole, with no overlay: Foundation ships sensible defaults, your fork edits
# them in place.
Unix/aerospace/aerospace.toml                                  merge=ours
Btop/Unix/btop.conf                                            merge=ours

# Payloads mirrored from WinuX (generic templates there too).
Git/.gitconfig                                                 merge=ours
NuGet/nuget.config                                             merge=ours
Firefox/user.js                                                merge=ours

# Custom-area docs landing page - upstream ships it saying "None yet"; your fork lists its own
# custom pages there. (Custom commands and per-module custom pages need no attribute: upstream
# never tracks those paths.)
docs/foundation/custom/README.md                                     merge=ours
